               <msContents>
                  <summary>Maḥzor according to the Western Asheknazi rite, for the High Holidays and
                     Sukkot</summary>
                  <msItem>
                     <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3329">מחזור מנהג אשכנז המערבי לימים נוראים ולסוכות</title>
                     <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3329">Maḥzor minhag Ashkenaz ha-maʿaravi le-yamim
                        noraʾim ṿele-Sukkot</title>
                     <note>Used by <persName>Daniel Goldschmidt</persName> in his edition of the
                        High Holidays and Sukkot services (Jerusalem, 1970-81). According to
                        Goldschmidt, this manuscript represents the late version of the rite and
                        includes many characteristic variant readings.</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <physDesc>
                  <objectDesc form="codex">
                     <supportDesc>
                        <support>parchment</support>
                        <extent n="291">ff. 291<dimensions type="leaf" unit="mm">
                              <height>354</height>
                              <width>258</width>
                           </dimensions></extent>
                        <condition><locus>Ff. 1-2, 289-291</locus> are heavily stained. The side
                           margin of <locus>f. 71</locus> has been cut off and subsequently repaired
                           with a strip of sewn-on parchment. The bottom margins of <locus>ff. 209,
                              210</locus> have been cut off.</condition>
                     </supportDesc>
                  </objectDesc>
                  <handDesc>
                     <summary>Ashkenazic square script</summary>
                     <handNote>The names <persName>Yehudah <foreign xml:lang="he"
                           >יהודה</foreign></persName> (<locus>f. 194r</locus>) and <persName>Itzḥaḳ
                              <foreign xml:lang="he">יצחק</foreign></persName> (<locus>ff. 40v,
                           195r</locus>) have been highlighted by means of tiny drawings: a lion
                        wearing a Jewish hat (<locus>f. 194r</locus>) next to the name Yehudah, and
                        a hand holding a flower (<locus>f. 40v</locus>) and a ram next to a tree
                           (<locus>f. 195r</locus>) next to the name Itzḥaḳ.</handNote>
                  </handDesc>
                  <decoDesc>
                     <p>On <locus>f. 79v</locus>, an unfinished drawing (by the scribe himself,
                        considering the unusual layout of the text) of a doorway framing the text
                        block.</p>
                     <p>The majority of the catchwords (alongside the personal names discussed
                        above) are decorated with tiny drawings of animals, some of which wear
                        Jewish hats.</p>
                  </decoDesc>
                  <additions>Some marginal notes, containing explanations of words and <hi
                        rend="italic">minhagim</hi> of <placeName key="place_108">Worms</placeName>. </additions>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <p>Vellum over thick paper boards. The spine bears the current shelfmark (167;
                        handpainted in black), and the former shelfmark (1070; printed on a slip of
                        paper).</p>
                  </bindingDesc>
               </physDesc>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate>[late 14th cent.]</origDate>
                     <origPlace>[Germany]</origPlace>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance>The names <persName>Menaḥem b. Yehudah <foreign xml:lang="he">מנחם בן
                           יהודה</foreign></persName>, <persName>Mosheh b. Yehudah <foreign
                           xml:lang="he">משה בן יהודה</foreign></persName>, and <persName>Uri b.
                        Yehudah <foreign xml:lang="he">אורי בן יהודה</foreign></persName> are
                     mentioned in the <hi rend="italic">reshut</hi> for the <hi rend="italic">Ḥatan
                        Bereshit</hi> in the Simḥat Torah liturgy; it is quite possible that these
                     were sons of the original owner, since the name <persName>Yehudah <foreign
                           xml:lang="he">יהודה</foreign></persName> was pointed out (<locus>f.
                        194r</locus>) alongside the name <persName>Itzḥak <foreign xml:lang="he"
                           >יצחק</foreign></persName> (<locus>ff. 40v, 195r</locus>) which may have
                     been the name of the scribe.</provenance>
                  <acquisition>It belonged to the library of <persName key="person_7753200">David
                        Oppenheim</persName> (<date calendar="Gregorian">1664-1736</date>), which
                     was purchased by the Bodleian Library in <date calendar="Gregorian"
                     >1829</date>.</acquisition>
               </history>
